forum.best_answer_content:
Thanks to everyone for every pointed direction. I finally succeeded after figuring out a few mistakes. First, at the beginning, I only had my engraver as a device to test my host. Application\_Ready started triggering ON after I decided to create my own device using a BluePill. Next, I had assumed that my USB\_OTG\_FS\_VBUS (PA9) would power my VBUS. But in reality, PA9 and VBUS must be powered by a 5-volt source.

Then, the callback name is USBH\_CDC\_ReceiveCallback, and the while (1) loop looks like this

```

while (1)

{

/* USER CODE END WHILE */

MX_USB_HOST_Process();

if (Appli_state == APPLICATION_READY && !Once)

{

HAL_UART_Printf(huart2, "Application Ready V2\r\n");

HAL_UART_Printf(huart2, "Sending Homing\r\n");

USBH_StatusTypeDef Result;

Result = USBH_CDC_Transmit(&hUsbHostFS, (uint8_t*)"$h\r\n", 6);

HAL_UART_Printf(huart2, "Transmit Home Result : %d\r\n", Result);

USBH_CDC_Receive(&hUsbHostFS, CDC_RX_Buffer, sizeof(CDC_RX_Buffer));

Once = 1;

}

}

```

The rest is identical to the introduction.

After that, I was back to square one with my laser engraver. Application\_Ready still wasn’t triggering. After modifying USBH\_CDC\_InterfaceInit

```

extern UART_HandleTypeDef huart2;

static USBH_StatusTypeDef USBH_CDC_InterfaceInit(USBH_HandleTypeDef *phost)

{

USBH_StatusTypeDef status;

uint8_t interface;

CDC_HandleTypeDef *CDC_Handle;

for (int i = 0; i < USBH_MAX_NUM_INTERFACES; i++)

{

HAL_UART_Printf(huart2, "IF=%d CLASS=%02X SUB=%02X PROT=%02X\r\n",

i,

phost->device.CfgDesc.Itf_Desc[i].bInterfaceClass,

phost->device.CfgDesc.Itf_Desc[i].bInterfaceSubClass,

phost->device.CfgDesc.Itf_Desc[i].bInterfaceProtocol);

}

interface = USBH_FindInterface(phost, COMMUNICATION_INTERFACE_CLASS_CODE, ABSTRACT_CONTROL_MODEL, COMMON_AT_COMMAND);

```

I discovered that Creality was using protocol 0x00 instead of 0x01. So I modified usbh\_cdc.h

```

#define COMMON_AT_COMMAND                                       0x00U

```

Now this method is compatible with the Nucleo-STM32F411RE, and pins PA11 and PA12 are populated. I’m even thinking of modifying my breadboard and making the Nucleo the central processor with a custom 40-pin female header board @ DIP 40.

